Disclaimer: what I would like to do is write a thoughtful piece about
something I've been noticing and thinking about.  What I am doing is
writing when I'm about to go to bed because it's late and I'm very
tired.  Sorry, that's the way it goes sometimes.

I've been thinking for a long time about the heated discussions that
occur in this group about making the game easier, or making it harder,
or (dis)allowing particular approaches to the game.  Something just
gelled for me, and I realized that there's a reason that we have two
camps (at least) on these issues.  I thought that if I spoke up, I
would be able to tell from the flames or the apathy whether there's
anything to this...

I'm starting to see that we have at least two distinct kinds of people
who like to play in dungeons.  Not to put too fine a point on it, there's
the Indiana Jones types, who have lots of confidence, and have honed
their skills by figuring out (what they hope is) the optimal strategy
for each kind of situation, and who are willing to push themselves into
situations that call for excellence in each move, and understand that
there may be no such thing as a minor mistake.

In case you're wondering, I'm not one of those.  I admire them, they're
lots of fun to watch at certain points, and they do get the adrenalin
moving...

Then, there's me.  I'm not going to assume that all the people who want
the game a bit easier are like me, but some of them may be.  I play this
stuff to relax, partly.  I like to be able to take a rest: this means
that I like to be able to gather enough stuff, earn enough power (whatever
the particular game allows) so that I can expect to have an easy time of
it.  It's my system, so for most games, I've figured out what I have to
do to be able to restore copies of save files (to h*ll with what the
designers put in there, I think it's dumb to have to start over and do
all that boring stuff).

I am one of those who, having heard of the existence of the dungeon, would
not ever have gone in there as a 1st level fighter in the first place.  One
of the dumbest things about HACK, NETHACK and friends for me is that the
game is over when you leave.  If it were real, I would be going back for
a shower and rest at every chance.  I might collect all those gems and
hire me some lackeys to try out unidentified objects.

I don't need to do things the hard way.  I would like to think I'm a bit
like the folks who wind up taking over so many companies from the founders
after the founders have found that they would rather start companies than
run them well.  I don't need the adrenalin hit: I usually play this stuff
after a full day, and my attention is simply not up to it.

I like to think that Indiana Jones would find the place, get the best few
of the treasures, and gain the glory.  Then I would go in and get well and
truly rich by mining the WHOLE thing, selling the spell research rights to
a consortium of wizards, and turn the thing into a theme park.

I know, I know, that appalls some folks.  That's entirely my point.  There's
more than one kind of person who likes to play these games.

Personally, I like loopholes.  I don't mind not dying.  I think it's ghastly
stupid to have to go back and start over when your main crime was not knowing
how feature "X" worked, when any sensible person would not have gone down
in any place that has killed so many without doing some serious research.
I think I ought to be able to take things out of the dungeon and ask the
local sages about them.  I think there would be things I could do to find
out about all sorts of stuff without using it up or committing suicide with
it.

I also like to think I'm one of the careful ones, who might just live to
come home to his family.  But not the kind of careful that watches every
move, never uses uppercase movement, and all the rest.  My idea of careful
is to go down in a new place with what the military calls a reconnaisance
in force: a whole bunch of heavy weaponry sent by an absentee general.

This is not everyone's idea of fun.  Fine.  It just occurred to me that
the dialog about these things was being conducted with the unspoken
assumption that there was a single best way to configure the game.  I do
not hold with that idea.  I think there's a lot of different kinds of
people, and there are good reasons for them to have different preferences.

I would like to see things like HARD get more detail; like whether polymorph
can destroy objects in the pile, whether it acts on more than one thing in
the pile, whether shops are ever locked, etc...

BTW, I don't much care what goes on the scoreboard, put anything you like
on it about how I cheat, but record my results please.  


